  "activity": {
    "id": "act_heart_anatomy_blood_flow_linear",
    "name": "Human Heart Anatomy and Blood Flow: Linear AR Lab",
    "description": "A simplified undergraduate AR lab for Human Anatomy and Physiology I. Students inspect intact and cutaway heart models, identify chambers, valves, and major vessels, trace pulmonary and systemic circulation, compare ventricular wall thickness, and explain how valves prevent backflow.",
    "workplace": "wp_heart_dual_circulation",
    "start": "step_01_orientation",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_01_orientation",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "1. Orient the Heart Models",
          "description": "Observe the intact exterior heart and the cutaway heart side by side. The apex points downward and slightly toward the patient's left. Remember: the patient's right side appears on your left when viewing the heart from the front. Learning objective: orient the heart anatomically before identifying structures."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_exterior_heart_model",
              "augmentation": "aug_label_orientation_title",
              "poi": "label_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_lab_table",
              "augmentation": "aug_audio_orientation",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_to_structure_tour"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_02_structure_tour",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "2. Identify Chambers, Valves, and Major Vessels",
          "description": "Use the cutaway model to locate the right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, and left ventricle; then identify the tricuspid, pulmonary, mitral/bicuspid, and aortic valves; finally locate the venae cavae, pulmonary arteries, pulmonary veins, and aorta. Learning objective: name the structural inventory of the heart."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_cutaway_heart_model",
              "augmentation": "aug_label_structure_tour",
              "poi": "label_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_cutaway_heart_model",
              "augmentation": "aug_image_structure_map",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_lab_table",
              "augmentation": "aug_audio_structure_tour",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_to_dual_circuits"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_03_dual_circuits",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "3. Distinguish Pulmonary and Systemic Circuits",
          "description": "Blue flow indicates deoxygenated blood returning from the body and moving to the lungs. Red flow indicates oxygenated blood returning from the lungs and moving to the body. Learning objective: distinguish the right-sided pulmonary circuit from the left-sided systemic circuit."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_cutaway_heart_model",
              "augmentation": "aug_anim_dual_circuit_flow",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_cutaway_heart_model",
              "augmentation": "aug_label_dual_circuit",
              "poi": "label_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_lab_table",
              "augmentation": "aug_audio_dual_circuit",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "thing_rbc_flow_marker"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_04_rbc_trace",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "4. Trace One Blood Cell Through the Full Path",
          "description": "Follow the animated cell from the superior vena cava to the right atrium, through the tricuspid valve to the right ventricle, through the pulmonary valve to the lungs, back via pulmonary veins to the left atrium, through the mitral valve to the left ventricle, and out the aortic valve and aorta. Learning objective: state the complete blood-flow sequence in order."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_rbc_flow_marker",
              "augmentation": "aug_anim_rbc_trace",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_rbc_flow_marker",
              "augmentation": "aug_label_rbc_sequence",
              "poi": "label_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_lab_table",
              "augmentation": "aug_audio_rbc_trace",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_to_wall_thickness"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_05_wall_thickness",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "5. Compare Right and Left Ventricular Wall Thickness",
          "description": "Compare the thinner right ventricular myocardium with the thicker left ventricular myocardium. The right ventricle pumps to the nearby low-pressure lungs; the left ventricle pumps to the whole systemic circuit at higher pressure. Learning objective: relate ventricular wall thickness to pump workload."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_ventricle_cross_sections",
              "augmentation": "aug_anim_ventricle_wall_compare",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_ventricle_cross_sections",
              "augmentation": "aug_label_wall_thickness",
              "poi": "label_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_lab_table",
              "augmentation": "aug_audio_wall_thickness",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "thing_valve_function_model"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_06_valve_function",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "6. Explain How Valves Prevent Backflow",
          "description": "Examine open and closed valve states. The atrioventricular valves\u2014tricuspid and mitral\u2014prevent backflow into atria during ventricular contraction. The semilunar valves\u2014pulmonary and aortic\u2014prevent backflow into ventricles during relaxation. Learning objective: identify valve locations and describe their anti-backflow function."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_valve_function_model",
              "augmentation": "aug_anim_valves_open_closed",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_valve_function_model",
              "augmentation": "aug_label_valve_function",
              "poi": "label_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_lab_table",
              "augmentation": "aug_audio_valve_function",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_to_recap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_07_recap",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "7. Recap the Five Core Concepts",
          "description": "Review: structural inventory, dual-circuit organization, complete blood-flow path, ventricular wall-thickness asymmetry, and valve prevention of backflow. Learning objective: consolidate the heart anatomy and circulation concepts for later lab assessment."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_summary_zone",
              "augmentation": "aug_image_recap_summary",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_summary_zone",
              "augmentation": "aug_label_dual_circuit",
              "poi": "label_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_lab_table",
              "augmentation": "aug_audio_recap",
